Cell["\<\
Now in theory, if I can manipulate C++\[OpenCurlyQuote]s normal distribution \
to make this distribution work, we should be good to go. The standard normal \
distribution does not have a constant offset however. So I should see if I \
can use some stats tricks to artificially construct my offset normal \
distribution. \
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.794072429972011*^9, 3.7940724638424473`*^9}, {
  3.7940725267475104`*^9, 
  3.794072540249025*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a300f2b7-7160-4a71-be4d-\
f959aeadba0d"],

Cell["\<\
Conclusion: Since C++ does not have a built in Gaussian distribution, this \
fitting does not amount too much. I will continue using this Mathematica \
Notebook to determine a good anisotropy potential\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7941472416988306`*^9, 
  3.794147281706188*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2fdeaa6c-e2ff-4d54-b8d3-\
69118010024a"],

Cell["\<\
Goals: Find a potential that will allow me to control the directions of \
faster/ slower growth and the strength of anisotropy.
\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.7941473215152073`*^9, 
  3.794147352724389*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2b4bcf7c-dbf0-4da7-bd87-\
5c9c17ab4fcc"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

